Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), one class of highly ordered crystalline coordination polymer with well-defined porous network, are formed by metal ion/cluster and organic ligands.[1, 2] Due to the diversity in composition/structure, high surface area, chemical/thermal stability, etc., MOFs have achieved versatile opportunities in broad fields including gas separation/storage, sensor, and catalysis.[3-8] Recently, MOFs have been rising as a fascinating family of electrocatalysts with both homogeneous and heterogeneous advantages: the well-defined structures and readily accessible active sites can be tuned at the molecular level to improve the catalytic performance and also enable the MOFs to serve as model systems for fundamental understanding of catalytic mechanism. Recently, various MOFs, such as $[\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\mathsf{O H})(\mathsf{b p y})_{2}(\mathsf{b t c})_{3}]$ , $[\mathsf{F e}_{2}(\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{t c p p}))\mathsf{C l}]$ , $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}(\mathsf{b t c})_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{3}]$ and $[\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{m i m})_{2}]$ , have been explored as oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) electrocatalysts.[2,9-12] However, great challenges still plagued on the conventional MOFs including electrical insulators and blockage of metal centers by organic ligands, resulting in their inferior activity (half-wave potential $\mathsf{E}_{1/2}\ <0.8\ \vee$ vs RHE) and dramatically limit their broader applications in electrocatalysis. Pyrolysis of MOFs into metal- and heteroatom-doped porous carbons has been widely demonstrated to improve the catalytic activity.[2,13,14] However, this method has to sacrifice the welldefined molecular active sites.[15]
In order to address these challenges, development of conductive two-dimensional (2D) conjugated MOFs, i.e., atomically ordered, planar metal-organic networks along 2D directions with fully in-plane $\pi$ delocalization and weak out-plane $\pi\cdot\pi$ stacking, is of great significance in electrocatalysis. This is due to their unique 2D features as well as improved electron transfer capacity and high utilization of exposed active sites apart from the inherited advantages of traditional MOFs.[13, 14, 16-24] Particularly, such MOFs can provide an ideal platform for mechanism investigations of the ORR process, which is essential for rational electrocatalyst design.[25-27] Recently, a $N\dot{\mathsf{I}}_{3}$ (hexaiminotriphenylene)2 2D MOF with $N i-N_{4}$ active sites was demonstrated as ORR electrocatalyst with onset potential of 0.82 V vs. RHE in alkaline media. The 2D porous structure ensures the fast mass transport while the defined active sites $(N i-N_{4})$ effectively catalyze the ORR.[17] Apart from the $M-N_{x}$ ,[28] the catalytic activity of ${\mathsf{M}}{\cdot}{\mathsf{O}}_{\mathsf{x}}$ sites with unique electronic structures is intrinsically high for electrocatalytic ORR.[29] For instance, perovskites (e.g. $\mathsf{L a C O}_{3})$ and $A B_{2}O_{4}$ spinel with $\mathsf{C o-O_{x}}$ sites possessing a single electron occupation of the $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{e}}_{9}$ orbital of Co atom show comparable ORR performance with platinum-based electrocatalysts.[29-30] Such $\mathsf{C o-O_{x}}$ sites effectively promote the breaking of the $M{\cdot}O H^{-}$ bond as well as the ${\mathsf{O}}_{2}{\mathsf{^{2-}/{\mathsf{O}}H}}^{}$ exchange reaction.[29-31] Therefore, incorporating molecular $\mathsf{C o-O_{x}}$ complexes structures within the highly conductive 2D conjugated MOFs hints at the promise for achieving superior catalytic ORR activity for fuel cells and metal-air batteries.
Herein, we synthesized 2,3,9,10,16,17,23,24-octahydroxy phthalocyaninato copper $(P{\mathsf{c C u-(O H)}}_{8})$ as the building block to construct copper-phthalocyanine-based 2D conjugated MOF with cobalt-bis(dihydroxy) complexes $({\mathsf{C o}}-{\mathsf{O}}_{4})$ as linkages $(P C C u-O_{8},$ - Co) by solvothermal method. Such 2D MOF $P_{C}C u-O_{8}$ -Co features a highly crystalized structure and optimized spin state of the cobalt node, wherein the unpaired electron in the $\propto^{\star}$ anti-bonding orbital $(\mathsf{e}_{9}\mathbf{=}1)$ in cobalt node enables $C o_{4}$ centers with high ORR activity.[27-29] The as-prepared $P_{C}C\cup-C_{8}.$ -Co, hybridized with carbon nanotubes $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}-C o/C N T s}$ ) affords superior ORR activity $\scriptstyle\cdot\mathsf{E}_{1/2=0.83}\lor$ vs. RHE, $\scriptstyle{\mathfrak{n}}=3.{\mathfrak{g}}3$ , and $j_{L=5.3\ m A\ \mathsf{c m^{-2}}},$ ) in alkaline media compared with other reported intrinsic MOF catalysts due to high coverage of electrochemically active Co sites, high conductivity and porous structure. The in-situ Raman spectroscopy was employed to probe the $\mathsf{C o-O}_{4}$ centers and their interaction with the oxygen intermediates during the ORR process, verifying that the Co-O units act as catalytic active sites. Finally, we employed $P_{C}C u-O_{8}$ -Co in the zinc-air battery, which exhibited promising discharge performance and capacity, superior to the commercial ${\sf P t}/{\sf C}$ .
$P_{C C\cup-C_{8}}$ -Co 2D conjugated MOF (Figure 1a) was synthesized via linking PcCu- $(\mathsf{O H})_{8}$ monomers by square planar, conjugated ${\mathsf{C o}}{\mathsf{-O}}_{4}$ linkages (Scheme S1). The successful preparation of $P_{C}C u-O_{8}.$ -Co was confirmed by Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) and powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) measurements (Figure S1 and 1b). Compared with the monomer $(P\mathsf{c C u-(O H)}_{8})$ , the peak of the C-O stretching vibration shifts from 1288 (C-O-H) to $1270\ c m^{-1}$ (C-O-Co) due to the Co-O bond formation in PcCu- ${{\mathrm O}_{8}}$ -Co accompanied by the disappearance of the peak of the C-O-H bending mode at $630~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ .[32] XRD pattern of $P_{C C\cup-C_{8}}$ -Co reveals a highly crystalline structure with prominent (100), (110), (200) and (001) peaks at 5.1o, $7.2^{\circ}$ $10.1^{\circ}$ and $27.5^{\circ}$ (Figure 1b), respectively. These intensive peaks indicate that $P C C u-O_{8}$ -Co exhibits a long-range order within the ab plane and layer stacking structure along the $c$ direction (001).[33, 34] We performed DFT calculations to optimize the atomic structure of single layer 2D MOF and then simulated different stacking arrangements of layer-stacked $P_{C C u-C_{8}}$ -Co, e.g., AA, AA-inclined, AA-serrated and AB stacking modes. The DFT results show that AA-serrated stacking geometry is energetically favored. The calculated Power-XRD pattern agrees well with the experimental result (Figure 1b). High-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) image (Figures 1c-d) and the corresponding selected area (electron) diffraction image (inset Figure 1c) manifest a $P_{C C\cup-C_{8}}$ -Co architecture based on a repeated unit size of $\sim1.75\ \mathsf{n m}$ . Besides, TEM and scanning electron microscope (SEM) images (Figure 1c and S2-3) display stacked nanosheets in $P C C u-O_{8}$ -Co. The corresponding element mapping analysis indicates a homogeneous distribution of Cu, Co, N, O and C elements over the whole structure (Figure S4).
The porous property of $P C C u-O_{8}$ -Co was evaluated by lowpressure ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption measurement at $77\mathsf{K}.$ . As shown in Figure 1e, a sharp uptake in the low relative pressure and a relative loose at $0.6\substack{-1.0\mathsf{P}/\mathsf{P}_{0}}$ are observed for $P C C u-O_{8}$ -Co with a Brunauer– Emmett–Teller surface area of $412\ m^{2}/{\mathfrak{g}}$ . According to the poresize distribution, the presence of micro-pore $(1.5\mathsf{n m})$ and mesopore $(2-10\mathsf{n m})$ is evident in PcCu-O8-Co, which are both favorable for the $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ and electrolyte transport during the ORR process. XPS analysis (Figure 1f and S5) was performed to investigate the composition of $P_{C}C u-O_{8}-C O$ , revealing the presence of Cu, Co, N, O and C elements in the sample. The highresolution Cu 2p XPS spectrum shows two sets of peaks at 936.7 and $953.8~\mathsf{e V}$ , assignable to Cu $2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2}$ and Cu $2p_{1/2}$ , respectively (Figure 1f) revealing the oxidation state of $+2$ for $\mathtt{C u}$ .[35, 36] The binding energy of Co $2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2}$ and Co $2p_{1/2}$ is observed at 781.6 and $796.9\:\mathsf{e V}$ , respectively, which suggests the presence of Co (II).[18, 37] The satellite peaks appear at the high energy side of Co $2p_{3/2}$ and Co $2p_{1/2}$ levels, respectively, revealing the antibonding orbital between Co atom and $\scriptscriptstyle\textcircled{\scriptsize{1}}$ atom.[35, 38] Importantly, such phenomenon also suggests that the electronic configuration of Co is relevant to $\mathfrak{t}_{2\mathfrak{g}}{^6\mathfrak{e}_{\mathfrak{g}}}^{1}$ , which is near the optimal $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{e}}_{9}$ filling of 1 and favorable for high electrocatalytic activity toward ORR.[29, 30]
The chemical states of Co and Cu atoms in $P_{C}C u-O_{8}$ -Co that were further determined by the $\mathsf{X}$ -ray absorption near edge structure (XANES) analysis (Figure 2a).[39-41] Cu K-edge of PcCu- $\phantom{0}{}$ -Co and the monomer $\{{\mathsf{P c C u-(O H)}}_{8}\}$ present similar spectra, resembling the reference compound (Copper(II) phthalocyanine: PcCu) due to the same common Cu-N motif. These spectra distinctly differ to those of Cu foil and $\mathtt{C u O}$ , confirming the Cu-N bonding in $P C C u-O_{8}$ -Co structure. In the Co K-edge spectra, the pre-edge at $7709\Theta\mathsf{V}$ originates from the absorption transition from 1s to 3d. Due to the electronic transition from 1s to $4{\mathsf p}$ , the strong absorption peak (white line) appears, which is the typical feature of Co atom with different Co-O environments and oxidation states.[4 $),42]\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}}$ -Co, CoO and $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ show strong absorption intensity in white line, while Co foil exhibits a very weak white line feature (Figure 2b).[40, 41] In addition, the absorption peak of white line of $P_{C C\cup-C_{8}}$ -Co locates before that of $\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ and close to CoO, indicating its oxidation valence of Co atom as $+2$ , which is consistent with XPS results. In order to investigate the electronic configuration of Co and the magnetic properties of $P C C u-O_{8}\cdot C O$ , high-field electron spin resonance (ESR) and static magnetization M measurements were performed (Figures 2c-d and S6).[42] In the representative ESR spectrum (Figure 2c), the main peak with the $g$ -factor $g{=}2.04$ and a shoulder with $\scriptstyle g=2.16$ correspond to the $\mathsf{C u}(\mathsf{I I})$ ions while the less pronounced features with $g{=}2.19$ and 2.05 can be tentatively assigned to ESR of $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{I I})$ ions in the lowspin state $S{=}1/2$ (see SI for details). The analysis of static magnetic susceptibility (Figure 2d) reveals the Curie constant $C{=}1.8$ emuOe-1mol-1K indicating that the major part of the $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{I I})$ ions in PcCu- ${\mathsf{O}}_{8}.$ -Co is in the low spin state $S{=}1/2$ (see SI for details). It is thus clear that the one unpaired electron is in $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{e}}_{9}$ orbital of Co $(\mathfrak{t}_{29}{}^{6}\mathfrak{e}_{9}{}^{1})$ for the 2D MOF, accelerating the ORR kinetics.[29]
Next, we mixed the synthetic PcCu- ${{\mathrm{\cdot}}{{\mathrm{O}}_{8}}}$ -Co with CNTs $(w/\mathsf{w}=2/1)$ ) as ORR electrocatalyst in alkaline media, wherein CNTs are used to improve the overall conductivity of the ORR electrode. Firstly, Cyclic voltammetry (CV) was conducted for assessing the ORR catalytic activity of $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}}\mathrm{-Co/CNT}$ . When the electrolyte was saturated with $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ , a typical cathodic peak at $0.83\mathrm{\:V}$ vs. RHE was observed in Figure 3a, pronouncing its high ORR activity. Furthermore, the catalytic activity of $P C C\cup-C_{8}$ - $\mathsf{C o/C N T}$ was evaluated by ORR polarization measurements. Figure $36$ shows the half-wave potential $\left(\mathsf{E}_{1/2}\right)$ of $P C C u-O_{8}.$ - $\mathsf{C o/C N T}$ is 0.83 V vs. RHE, which is close to the commercial $\mathsf{P t/C}$ (Figure S7, E $:_{1/2}=0.85$ V vs. RHE). Furthermore, the contrast samples including PcCu- $({\mathsf{O H}})_{8}/{\mathsf{C N T}}$ and $P C C u-O_{8}$ -M/CNT $\scriptstyle\mathrm{M=Fe}$ ， Cu and Ni) were synthesized and evaluated for ORR (Figure 3c,
S8, and Table S1). PcCu- $-({\mathsf{O H}})_{8}/{\mathsf{C N T}}$ presented inferior $\mathsf{E}_{1/2}$ (0. 63 V vs. RHE) compared to that of $P C C\cup-O_{8}$ -Ni/CNT (0.67 V vs. RHE), $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}-C u/C N T}$ (0.69 V vs. RHE), $P C C u-O_{8}$ -Fe/CNT $_{0.75\vee}$ vs. RHE) and $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}-C o/C N T}$ due to the saturated $C u N_{4}$ is inert in electrocatalytic ORR,[43-45] suggesting $C u N_{4}$ sites were not the dominant while $M O_{4}$ are active center. Notably, the 2D MOF with $\mathsf{C o O}_{4}$ units show the best ORR performance among these samples, implying the superiority of optimal $\mathsf{C o O}_{4}$ with the presence of single $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{e}}_{9}$ in enhancing the ORR catalytic activity. To investigate the effects of CuN4, we also synthesized $P C-O_{8}$ - $\mathsf{C o/C N T}$ (Figure S9-10). It exhibited lower $\mathsf{E}_{1/2}$ (0. $70~\mathsf{V}$ vs. RHE) compared to $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}-C o/C N T s}$ (Figure 3c), revealing the synergistic effects of $C u N_{4}$ and $\mathsf{C o O}_{4}$ in promoting the catalytic activity of $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}-C o/C N T}$ . Moreover, the high ORR catalytic activity of $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}-C o/C N T}$ is also gleaned from its large limited current density $(5.3\ m A\ c m^{-2})$ at 1600 rpm, which is much better than the currently reported MOFs and comparable with the other cobalt related electrocatalysts (Table S2), thus revealing the superiority of 2D MOF as ORR electrocatalyst. The high ORR performance is attributed to its highly ordered and 2D conjugated porous structure as well as abundant exposed active Co-O sites ( $\Gamma_{0}=34.2$ umol cm-2, Figure S11). Its Tafel slope (Figure S12a) was calculated to be $61\mathrm{~mV}\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ at low current density, similar to that of $\mathsf{P t/C}$ ? $(68\ \mathsf{m V}\ \mathsf{d e c}^{-1}.$ ), indicating that transfer of the first electron catalyzed by $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}-C o/C N T}$ is the rate-determining step at room temperature as the Tafel slope is closed to - 2.303RT/F $(\approx60~\mathsf{m V}$ dec-1, R, T, a and F are the molar gas constant, absolute temperature, transfer coefficient and Faraday constant, respectively).[46]
To further investigate ORR kinetics, rotating disc electrode (RDE) measurement at different rotating speeds were carried out and the corresponding Koutecký–Levich (K-L) plots were acquired. Inset figure 3b shows the linear and near parallel K-L plots at different applied potentials, implying a first-order reaction kinetics toward the concentration of dissolved oxygen and a similar electron transfer number (n) at $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}-C o/C N T}.$ [47] n is calculated 3.93 at $0.4~\mathsf{V}$ vs. RHE, suggesting that PcCu-O8- $\mathsf{C o/C N T}$ prefers a 4e oxygen reduction process. The corresponding kinetic current density ( $J_{\mathrm{k},}$ Figure S12b) is $20.1~\mathsf{m A}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , which is even higher than that of Pt/C $(14\ m A\ \mathsf{c m^{-2}})$ , indicating its intrinsic superior ORR activity. More importantly, only little changes of $\mathsf{E}_{1/2}$ ( $(16~\mathsf{m V})$ were found for $P C C u-O_{8}.$ - $\mathsf{C o/C N T}$ after 5000 ORR cycles, while $\mathsf{P t/C}$ showed $40~\mathsf{m V}$ shift of $\mathsf{E}_{1/2}$ , highlighting the better ORR stability of $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}-C o/C N T}$ (Figure 3d and S7). In addition, XRD and SEM measurements (Figure S13) of $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}-C o/C N T}$ after 5000 ORR cycles demonstrated that no obvious changes in its morphology and structure occurred, suggesting its excellent ORR stability.
As another contrast, CNT support (Figure S14) exhibited extremely low ORR catalytic activity in term of the $\mathsf{E}_{1/2}$ (0.62 V vs. RHE) and $\dot{\mu}$ $(2.6\ m A\ \mathsf{c m^{-2}})$ , revealing the role of CNTs on enhancing the effective electron transfer between $P C C u-O_{8}.$ - $\mathsf{C o/C N T}$ and RDE electrode. Figure S16b showed Electrochemical Impedance Spectroscopy (EIS) result suggests its sufficiently fast electron transfer during the ORR process. Due to the good catalytic activity of $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}-C o/C N T}$ in alkaline media, we further applied it as the air electrocatalyst for primary zinc-air battery. The assembled zinc-air battery showed superior performance with open circuit voltage of 1.37 V, discharge current density of $120\mathsf{m A}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ at $0.8\mathrm{\:V}$ and maximum power density of $94\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ (Figures 3e-f), which are superior to those of $\mathsf{P t/C}$ $(95.5~\mathsf{m A}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ and $76.4\mathrm{m}\mathrm{w}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, Figures S17-18), The capacity of the primary battery with $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}-C o/C N T}$ as air catalyst was about $700\mathrm{\mAh\9zn}^{-1}$ . And the constructed battery series were able to power LED (Light-emitting diode) lights for more than one week (Figure S19). Therefore, the high catalytic activity and stability of $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}-C o/C N T}$ , as well as its good performance in Zinc-air battery confirm its feasibility as effective ORR electrocatalyst.
Density functional theory (DFT) calculation illustrated that the Co sites in the linkages display high adsorption energy towards the intermediate OOH- during the ORR progress compared to CuN (Figures 4a and S20, Table S3), implying Co-O units as the ORR active sites.[47, 48] $I n$ -situ Raman spectro-electrochemistry was employed to monitor the reactions under turnover conditions. Figure 4b shows the Raman spectra at different applied potentials in $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ saturated 0.1 M KOH. The spectral pattern in the frequency range from 400 to $1600~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ of $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}-C o/C N T}$ are identical before $(0\lor\lor\{\mathsf{s}.\mathsf{A g}/\mathsf{A g C l})$ and after (0 V vs. Ag/AgCl) ORR testing, corroborating the high stability of $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8}-C o/C N T}$ . When the asprepared $\mathsf{P c C u-O_{8^{-}}C o/C N T}$ electrode was immersed into the alkaline electrolyte, two peaks appeared at 384 and $517~{\mathsf{c m}}^{-1}$ , which were ascribed to the adsorption of OH species on the cobalt center to form Co-OH species.[49] The intensity of these two peaks decreased with the increased applied overpotential (-0.2 to $\mathsf{-0.6V}$ vs. $\mathsf{A g}/\mathsf{A g C l}_{\mathsf{\ell}}^{\mathsf{\prime}}$ ), potentially originating from the interaction of the oxygen intermediates and cobalt metal center under turnover potentials. In addition, the peak at $451~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ , assigned to Co (II)- O, gradually disappeared with increase in overpotential due to conversion of Co (II) to $\mathsf{C o}(||\mathsf{I}|)$ species during the ORR reaction process.[50] Accordingly, a new peak at $691~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ was observed during the ORR process, which is assigned to the Co-O(III) oxygen intermediate.[51, 53] The intensity of this peak increased with the applied overpotential indicating much more accumulation of oxygen intermediates under larger overpotential. Therefore, the combination of the theoretical calculation and in-situ Raman spectro-electrochemistry corroborate that the Co-O centers in the PcCu- ${{\mathrm{\Omega}}}_{{{\mathrm{\Omega}}}}{{\mathrm{\Omega}}}_{{{\mathrm{\Omega}}}}$ -Co 2D MOF act as the catalytic active sites for ORR. A proposed ORR mechanism based on the derived data is shown in Figure 4d.
In conclusion, we have developed a 2D conjugated MOF. The well-defined Co-O centers with the specific electronic configuration (single occupied $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{e}}_{9}$ orbitals), highly ordered porous framework and 2D conjugated structures synergistically contribute to the high ORR performance, which is superior to other reported intrinsic MOFs electrocatalysts. Through theoretical modelling and in-situ Raman spectro-electrochemical studies, Co centers were verified as the catalytic sites. In addition, the as-prepared 2D MOF proved to work as ORR electrocatalyst in a zinc-air battery device with an excellent discharge performance and capacity, which is even superior to commercial $\mathsf{P t/C}$ systems. Our work highlights the 2D conjugated MOFs as effective electrocatalysts and verify that optimizing the architecture and electronic structure of 2D MOF is an effective strategy to develop highly active electrocatalyst toward ORR.
